## T-01a — A Class A database is detected as lacking the hash columns, rather than silently reported up to date

MUST FAIL FIRST. Two things are recorded here, both required to establish
the failure: (a) no detection mechanism exists in the repository at all,
so the acceptance criterion cannot currently be met by any code path;
and (b) the concrete failure mode the criterion guards against — a
Class A database passing through today's migration set with zero
indication it was ever missing the columns.

### (a) No detection tooling exists

$ find . -not -path "./target/*" -not -path "./.git/*" \( -iname "*migration*check*" -o -iname "*check*migration*" -o -iname "*class*a*" \)

(no output)

# exit code: 0, zero matches — there is no script, gate, or Rust code
# anywhere in the tree that inspects a database's schema class.

### (b) Today's tooling silently upgrades a Class A fixture with no record it happened

Class A fixture built from `git show 0.1.0:sql/0001_initial.sql` (tag
0.1.0, confirmed via `diff` against current sql/0001_initial.sql to
carry no prev_hash/row_hash/idx_audit_row_hash — the only substantive
difference is those three additions plus JA→EN comment translation).

$ sqlite3 t01a-classA.db < 0.1.0-0001_initial.sql
exit: 0
$ sqlite3 t01a-classA.db "PRAGMA table_info(audit_logs);" | grep -i hash
(no output — no hash columns, as expected for Class A)
$ sqlite3 t01a-classA.db < sql/0002_audit_hash_chain.sql
exit: 0
$ sqlite3 t01a-classA.db "PRAGMA table_info(audit_logs);" | grep -i hash
11|prev_hash|TEXT|0||0
12|row_hash|TEXT|0||0

# exit code: 0 throughout — the fixture silently gains the columns via
# 0002 today, with no diagnostic that it was ever a different class.
# This is the exact "silently reported up to date" failure mode named
# in the subject: once 0002 is deleted (Build step 1), the same
# fixture will have *no* forward migration and no diagnostic either —
# it will be reported up to date while still missing the columns.
# T-01a must therefore assert an explicit, positive report of the
# deficiency, not merely the absence of an error.

